# SpecKit Constitution: Shopping Application

## Project Overview
This constitution establishes the fundamental principles, standards, and requirements for the shopping application built with Next.js, TypeScript, and modern web technologies.

## 1. Code Quality Principles

### 1.1 TypeScript Standards
- **Strict Type Safety**: All code must use TypeScript with strict mode enabled
- **No `any` types**: Avoid using `any` type; use proper type definitions or `unknown` with type guards
- **Interface Consistency**: Use interfaces for object shapes and types for unions/primitives
- **Generic Types**: Leverage generics for reusable components and utilities
- **Type Exports**: Export all custom types and interfaces for reusability

### 1.2 Code Structure & Organization
- **Single Responsibility**: Each function/component should have one clear purpose
- **DRY Principle**: Eliminate code duplication through reusable utilities and components
- **Component Composition**: Favor composition over inheritance
- **Separation of Concerns**: Separate business logic, UI logic, and data handling
- **File Naming**: Use kebab-case for files, PascalCase for components, camelCase for functions

### 1.3 Testing Requirements
- **Unit Tests**: All utility functions must have unit tests (minimum 80% coverage)
- **Component Tests**: All React components must have corresponding test files
- **Integration Tests**: Critical user flows must have integration tests
- **E2E Tests**: Core shopping functionality must have end-to-end tests
- **Test Documentation**: Each test should clearly describe what it validates

### 1.4 Documentation Standards
- **JSDoc Comments**: All public functions and components must have JSDoc documentation
- **README Updates**: Keep README.md current with setup and deployment instructions
- **API Documentation**: Document all API endpoints and data structures
- **Component Props**: Document all component props with TypeScript and JSDoc
- **Inline Comments**: Use comments for complex business logic and algorithms

## 2. User Experience Consistency

### 2.1 Design System
- **Component Library**: Maintain a consistent component library with reusable UI elements
- **Design Tokens**: Use standardized colors, typography, spacing, and shadows
- **Responsive Design**: All components must work across mobile, tablet, and desktop
- **Accessibility**: Follow WCAG 2.1 AA guidelines for all user interfaces
- **Loading States**: Provide clear loading indicators for all async operations

### 2.2 Navigation & Flow
- **Intuitive Navigation**: Users should always know where they are and how to get back
- **Breadcrumbs**: Implement breadcrumbs for deep navigation paths
- **Search Functionality**: Provide robust search with filters and sorting options
- **Cart Persistence**: Shopping cart should persist across sessions
- **Error Handling**: Graceful error messages with clear recovery paths

### 2.3 Interactive Elements
- **Consistent Interactions**: Similar actions should behave the same way throughout the app
- **Feedback Systems**: Provide immediate feedback for user actions (success, error, loading)
- **Form Validation**: Real-time validation with clear error messages
- **Button States**: Clear visual states for buttons (default, hover, active, disabled)
- **Touch Targets**: Minimum 44px touch targets for mobile interactions

### 2.4 Content Standards
- **Consistent Tone**: Maintain a consistent voice and tone across all text
- **Clear Messaging**: Use plain language and avoid technical jargon
- **Error Messages**: Provide helpful, actionable error messages
- **Empty States**: Design meaningful empty states with clear next actions
- **Product Information**: Standardized product display with consistent information hierarchy

## 3. Performance Requirements

### 3.1 Core Web Vitals
- **Largest Contentful Paint (LCP)**: Must be under 2.5 seconds
- **First Input Delay (FID)**: Must be under 100 milliseconds
- **Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS)**: Must be under 0.1
- **First Contentful Paint (FCP)**: Target under 1.8 seconds
- **Time to Interactive (TTI)**: Target under 3.8 seconds

### 3.2 Asset Optimization
- **Image Optimization**: Use Next.js Image component with appropriate formats (WebP, AVIF)
- **Code Splitting**: Implement route-based and component-based code splitting
- **Bundle Size**: Keep JavaScript bundles under 250KB gzipped per route
- **Tree Shaking**: Eliminate unused code through proper imports
- **Font Loading**: Use font-display: swap and preload critical fonts

### 3.3 Caching Strategy
- **Static Assets**: Implement long-term caching for static assets (images, fonts, CSS)
- **API Responses**: Cache API responses appropriately with proper invalidation
- **CDN Usage**: Utilize CDN for static asset delivery
- **Browser Caching**: Implement proper cache headers for different asset types
- **Service Workers**: Consider implementing service workers for offline functionality

### 3.4 Database & API Performance
- **Query Optimization**: Optimize database queries to minimize response times
- **Pagination**: Implement pagination for large data sets
- **Rate Limiting**: Implement rate limiting to prevent abuse
- **Response Compression**: Use gzip/brotli compression for API responses
- **Connection Pooling**: Implement database connection pooling

### 3.5 Monitoring & Analytics
- **Performance Monitoring**: Implement real user monitoring (RUM)
- **Error Tracking**: Set up comprehensive error tracking and alerting
- **Core Web Vitals Tracking**: Monitor Core Web Vitals in production
- **Performance Budgets**: Set and enforce performance budgets in CI/CD
- **Regular Audits**: Conduct monthly performance audits using Lighthouse

## 4. Security Requirements

### 4.1 Data Protection
- **Input Validation**: Validate and sanitize all user inputs
- **SQL Injection Prevention**: Use parameterized queries and ORM protection
- **XSS Prevention**: Sanitize output and use Content Security Policy
- **CSRF Protection**: Implement CSRF tokens for state-changing operations
- **Authentication**: Use secure authentication methods (OAuth, JWT with proper validation)

### 4.2 Privacy & Compliance
- **Data Minimization**: Only collect necessary user data
- **GDPR Compliance**: Implement proper consent mechanisms and data deletion
- **Cookie Policy**: Clear cookie usage disclosure and consent
- **Third-party Scripts**: Audit and minimize third-party script usage
- **PII Handling**: Proper handling and protection of personally identifiable information

## 5. Development Workflow

### 5.1 Version Control
- **Git Flow**: Use feature branches with pull request reviews
- **Commit Messages**: Follow conventional commit format
- **Branch Protection**: Require reviews and passing tests before merging
- **Code Reviews**: All code must be reviewed by at least one other developer
- **Documentation**: Update documentation with significant changes

### 5.2 CI/CD Pipeline
- **Automated Testing**: All tests must pass before deployment
- **Code Quality Gates**: Lint, type check, and test coverage requirements
- **Performance Testing**: Automated performance regression testing
- **Security Scanning**: Automated security vulnerability scanning
- **Deployment Strategy**: Use blue-green or canary deployments for production

### 5.3 Environment Management
- **Environment Parity**: Keep development, staging, and production environments consistent
- **Configuration Management**: Use environment variables for configuration
- **Secret Management**: Secure handling of API keys and sensitive data
- **Database Migrations**: Automated and reversible database migrations
- **Dependency Management**: Regular dependency updates and security patches

## 6. Compliance & Quality Gates

### 6.1 Definition of Done
A feature is considered complete when it:
- Passes all automated tests (unit, integration, e2e)
- Meets accessibility requirements (WCAG 2.1 AA)
- Achieves performance benchmarks (Core Web Vitals)
- Has proper documentation and type definitions
- Passes security scan without critical vulnerabilities
- Is reviewed and approved by at least one team member

### 6.2 Quality Metrics
- **Test Coverage**: Minimum 80% code coverage
- **Performance Score**: Lighthouse score of 90+ for performance
- **Accessibility Score**: Lighthouse score of 100 for accessibility
- **Best Practices Score**: Lighthouse score of 100 for best practices
- **Bundle Size**: JavaScript bundle under 250KB gzipped per route

### 6.3 Review Process
- **Code Review Checklist**: Use standardized checklist for all reviews
- **Performance Review**: Include performance impact assessment
- **Security Review**: Assess security implications of changes
- **UX Review**: Validate user experience consistency
- **Documentation Review**: Ensure documentation is updated and accurate

## 7. Enforcement & Governance

### 7.1 Automated Enforcement
- **ESLint Rules**: Enforce code quality through linting rules
- **TypeScript Strict Mode**: Enforce type safety through compiler settings
- **Prettier**: Enforce consistent code formatting
- **Husky Git Hooks**: Pre-commit hooks for linting and testing
- **CI/CD Checks**: Automated checks in continuous integration pipeline

### 7.2 Regular Reviews
- **Weekly Code Quality Reviews**: Team review of code quality metrics
- **Monthly Performance Audits**: Comprehensive performance assessment
- **Quarterly Architecture Reviews**: Review system architecture and design decisions
- **Annual Constitution Review**: Update constitution based on learnings and industry changes

This constitution serves as the foundation for maintaining high standards in code quality, user experience, and performance throughout the development lifecycle of the shopping application.